# Card fixtures pair a JSON file with a PNG that embeds the SAME bytes
# in its chara chunk (TestSyntheticCard asserts byte-level equivalence
# through Card.Extensions, which is retained verbatim). CRLF checkout
# conversion — the default on Windows CI runners — would desync the
# pair, so pin the JSON to LF and mark the PNGs binary explicitly.
examples/cards/*.json text eol=lf
examples/cards/*.png binary

# ctrlproto golden frames are byte-compared against LF-encoded wire output
# (TestGoldenFrames); a CRLF checkout (the Windows CI default) desyncs every
# frame. Pin the goldens to LF.
packages/agent/ctrlproto/testdata/*.json text eol=lf

# The connproto golden corpus is the same shape and the same trap:
# TestGoldenFixtureMatchesCorpus byte-compares the published file against the
# in-source frames, so a CRLF checkout marks a correct fixture stale. This one
# is also PUBLISHED for connector authors to test against, which makes its line
# endings part of what is being distributed, not just a local build detail.
packages/agent/connproto/testdata/*.jsonl text eol=lf

# extproto publishes one too, for extension authors, and it arrived a release
# after connproto's — same shape, same test name, same trap, and unpinned. A
# CRLF checkout marks the fixture stale on Windows only, which is the release
# gate, which is the most expensive place to learn it. golden_pin_test.go now
# fails on the commit that adds an unpinned corpus instead.
packages/agent/extproto/testdata/*.jsonl text eol=lf

# i18n reference catalogs are byte-compared against the linter's LF
# output (`terva-i18n-lint -check`, the CI gate); a CRLF checkout (the
# Windows CI default) marks every catalog stale. Pin them to LF.
packages/i18n/locales/**/*.json text eol=lf

# Builtin personas are go:embed'ed at build time with whatever line
# endings the checkout produced, so a CRLF checkout (the Windows CI
# default) poisons the embedded bytes: charter tests match multi-line
# markers, and shipped prompt text should not vary by build host. Pin
# every embedded persona to LF.
#
# This path is a STRING, and the personas have moved once already: when the
# library became its own package the pin stayed pointing at the old directory,
# the charters came through a Windows checkout as CRLF, and the marker match
# failed for all seven review-crew charters. persona_pin_test.go joins this line
# to the directory persona.go actually embeds, so the next move fails a test
# instead of a release.
packages/agent/persona/personas/**/*.md text eol=lf

# Generated artifacts committed so a plain `go build -tags terva_web` needs no
# JS/Node toolchain (see `just web-build`). Mark them linguist-generated so the
# forges (Forgejo/GitHub, via go-enry/linguist) collapse them in PR diffs and
# omit them from language stats — a reviewer reads the source change, not the
# minified/hashed output. Orthogonal to the eol=lf pins above; this only affects
# review presentation, never checkout. `just diff-review` reads the same marker.
#   - the Vite build output (hashed+minified JS/CSS, index.html, sw.js, icons);
#   - the English-as-key reference catalogs the i18n linter regenerates
#     (`terva-i18n-lint`, en.json only — the fi.json translations are AUTHORED,
#     so they stay reviewable and are deliberately NOT marked);
#   - the first-paint web-catalog mirror the build copies into the client bundle.
packages/agent/web/client/dist/**            linguist-generated=true
packages/i18n/locales/**/en.json             linguist-generated=true
packages/agent/web/client/src/locales/**     linguist-generated=true
